  "result": "I withdrew the node rather than revising it, because the vocabulary can't express span f7. The withdrawal is in `record.jsonl` with kind `atom`.\n\n- **The span:** f7 is condition (c), \"When directed by an official traffic control device.\" It makes the lamp duty apply whenever a device directs lamp use, even when it isn't dark (f5) and visibility is fine (f6).\n- **Why the planned atoms don't work:** no `dev.regulatory_sign` code and no `dev.zone_sign` kind means \"display lighted lamps.\" `dev.governs` only says which regions a device controls, not what it tells drivers. Writing f7 with these atoms would turn the lamp duty on under any regulatory sign, such as a no-parking or one-way sign. That is a different condition from what the law says.\n- **Why there was no other way out:** a condition span can't be dropped or narrowed, which is why the gate refused the coder's `no_atom` declaration. It also can't be an assumption, since a sign on the road is something a source can observe, not an administrative category.\n- **What is missing:** a device atom that directs road users on the regions it governs to turn their lamps on, with the same conditions attribute as `dev.zone_sign`. The plan already proposed it as `dev.lamp_use_sign`. The withdrawal also lists the nearest existing atoms and the clause in each contract that rules it out.\n\nThe harness will file this as a gap, and the law stays blocked until the ontology has such an atom. The coder reported f1–f6 as written in the rule file. Its eight test scenes were never checked, because the gate stopped at f7.",
